@page "/expivot/sources"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Pivot


<DocsPage Product="ExPivot" Title="Sources"
          Lead="A Pivot Source is what a report is computed from: your records in the browser, a Snapshot read from a file, or a server that answers the same questions over your own transport.">

    <DocsSection Title="How a source is asked">
        <p>
            ExPivot requests a report Window and the metadata describing its full extent. Local data is
            calculated by the same engine that a server uses. A slow answer leaves the report under its loading
            indication, and a newer question cancels the older one, unless the older one is for a Window that
            already holds the rows now on screen. A question asked for newer data the source announced, or for a
            Window asked early while every row on screen is held, leaves the report undimmed. Copy and Selection Summary can include rows outside the Window,
            and name the Report Version they answer; Show Details names the Source Version the report was computed
            from.
        </p>
        <p>
            The Window asked for is the one the grid is scrolled to, with the rows the source reads ahead either
            side: none for a local report, 60 for a server's unless you say otherwise (below). When a layout, a
            collapse, a filter or newer data makes a report that ends before that Window does, ExPivot asks again for a Window of the same
            height ending at the new report's last row, and shows that one, as the grid moves its own view to the
            end of a result that shrank. The report you were reading stays on screen until it lands, and later
            questions ask for that Window until you scroll elsewhere. A source therefore needs nothing of its own
            for a shrinking report: it answers the Window it is asked for, with no rows when the report ends
            before it.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong><code>Source</code></strong> takes the Pivot Source: <code>PivotSource.From</code> over local records or a Snapshot, including a CSV that stays in the browser. ExPivot computes the report over it in the page, with the engine a server runs.</li>
            <li><strong><code>ReportSource</code></strong> takes a report source instead, a <code>PivotReportSource</code>, for a report a server computes. Its <code>Fetch</code> factory uses your delegates to request the server's report Windows and operations. Hand ExPivot one of the two, never both.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the source in a field.</strong> Each report owns independent calculation state; a new Pivot Source starts a new calculation, as a refresh.</li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Over your objects">
        <p>
            <code>PivotFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</code> declares each field once with a typed accessor
            (<code>Sales.cs</code> shows them all). The declaration makes both the Snapshot column the records
            are read into, without boxing a value, and the Pivot Field over it:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Text</strong>, with Excel's custom list (<code>itemOrder</code>) or an Order Key; <code>null</code> is the Item <code>(blank)</code>, and <code>""</code> is a value.</li>
            <li><strong>Number</strong> over <code>decimal</code> (exact), <code>int</code> or <code>long</code> (exact), or <code>double</code> (Excel's arithmetic).</li>
            <li><strong>Date</strong> over <code>DateTime</code>, <code>DateOnly</code> or <code>DateTimeOffset</code>, with <code>Year</code>, <code>Quarter</code> and <code>Month</code> as fields of their own, ordered by the calendar.</li>
            <li><strong>Boolean</strong>, labelled <code>TRUE</code> and <code>FALSE</code>; and a <strong>Key</strong>, the Record Key a Change Batch finds a record by.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            <code>PivotSource.From(records, fields)</code> reads the records on the calling thread. For a large
            list in a browser, <code>PivotSource.FromAsync</code> reads them in slices of about 30 ms, yielding
            between them and reporting progress, so the page keeps painting.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(LoadInSlices)" Mud="typeof(LoadInSlicesMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Over a Snapshot">
        <p>
            A <a href="data">Snapshot</a> is pivoted as it is, wherever it came from. <code>PivotSource.From(snapshot)</code>
            offers one field per column, captioned and typed by it; or name the fields you want over its columns,
            a date part naming the Date column it is a part of. A field naming a column the Snapshot does not
            have is refused by name.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FromSnapshot)" Mud="typeof(FromSnapshotMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="From a CSV">
        <p>
            <code>ExGrid.Data</code> reads a CSV straight into a Snapshot's columns under a <code>CsvSchema</code> you
            declare: what each column is and how to read it. Nothing is guessed, because a guess reads the account
            number <code>004417</code> as the number 4,417. A file the user brings comes from Blazor's
            <code>InputFile</code> (<code>e.File.OpenReadStream(maxAllowedSize)</code>); the Example writes one in
            memory instead.
        </p>
        <p>
            A value its column cannot read refuses the whole file, naming the row and the column. The report is
            then not drawn at all, rather than drawn over the rows that happened to read. See
            <a href="data">Snapshot</a> for the Schema's options and for suggesting one from a file nobody has
            described.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FromCsv)" Mud="typeof(FromCsvMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/SalesCsv.cs", "Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A server's source">
        <p>
            <code>PivotReportSource.Fetch</code> carries your Window, Items, Copy, Summary and Details transports.
            ExPivot ships no HTTP client or endpoint. <code>PivotReportJson</code> writes their questions and answers
            as versioned JSON, preserving decimal and double values. Keep a <code>PivotReportSource.From(data)</code>
            on the server. Its data can be a Snapshot, or a SQL-backed <code>PivotSource</code> that refreshes a full
            aggregate answer; the browser still receives only report Windows.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>UpdateMode</code> declares incremental changes or full refresh. The bundled Snapshot source is incremental:
                a notice that its data moved on asks for its changes. A full-refresh source, such as a SQL query that can only say
                that something changed, is asked to recompute its whole aggregate on every notice, so its server answers with the
                newest even when its Pivot Source cannot tell; the browser still receives only the Window and its Window Changes.
            </li>
            <li>Culture, formats and display words are explicit; custom Order Keys select policies registered on the server.</li>
            <li>
                <strong>Find reads the report through Copy.</strong> <kbd>Ctrl+F</kbd> searches every cell the report lays
                out, so the rows beyond the Window are asked of your <code>copy</code> transport, by the Report Version on
                screen, a block of rows at a time, until the match (<a href="expivot/layout#find">Find</a>).
            </li>
            <li>The Consumer owns transport and report-state disposal. A missing baseline is recovered automatically; an unavailable older operation refuses by name.</li>
            <li>
                <strong>A server's report is read ahead, and asked for early.</strong> Each round trip to the server would
                otherwise leave the rows a scroll brings in as placeholders until the answer lands. <code>Fetch</code>'s
                <code>readAheadRows</code>, 60 unless you pass another number, asks for that many rows either side of the
                rows on screen, clamped into the report, so ordinary scrolling and a key that moves a row are painted from
                rows already held. ExPivot also asks for the next Window ten rows before the one it holds runs out, while
                the rows on screen are still there, and keeps a question in flight while its Window holds them. Every row
                read ahead is a row of every column, in each answer and in each live update, so a wide report may want
                fewer, and a slow network more. <code>0</code> asks for exactly the rows on screen. A report computed in
                the page, <code>PivotSource.From</code> or <code>PivotReportSource.From</code>, has no round trip to hide
                and reads nothing ahead. A Window asked for early, while every row on screen is held, does not dim the
                report: nothing you see is waiting for it. Only rows on screen that wait for an answer bring the loading
                indication.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A server may answer fewer rows than it was asked for.</strong> One that caps its answers can send a
                complete Window shorter than the one asked, within it, as long as it holds the rows on screen: the answer is
                checked against those rows, not against the rows read ahead. ExPivot holds the Window it was sent, and asks
                for that Window, and Window Changes against it, from then on. An answer that does not reach the rows on
                screen, or lies outside the Window asked, is refused, naming the rows answered, asked and needed, and the
                report stays as it was. A cap below the read-ahead from the start of the Window asked can never reach the
                rows on screen: pass a smaller <code>readAheadRows</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                Once a Window is on screen, a server sends <strong>Window Changes</strong> in place of the whole Window: the rows that
                changed since the Window ExPivot holds, the Baseline Window, with the digest of the Window they make
                (<code>PivotReportUpdate.Changed</code>, <code>PivotReportUpdate.WindowDigest</code>). ExPivot applies them to the
                Window it holds and compares before showing anything; Window Changes that do not match, or name no digest, are
                discarded and the complete Window asked for instead. <code>PivotReportSource.From</code> builds complete Window
                Changes. If you build or relay them yourself, include every row whose shown values changed (subtotals, grand totals
                and percentages too) and compute the digest with <code>PivotReportDigest.Of</code> over your whole resulting Window,
                never over the rows you send.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ServerSource)" Mud="typeof(ServerSourceMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/SalesServer.cs", "Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            The server here is simulated in the page, with half a second of latency: change the layout and watch the
            report wait under its loading indication. Tick Date in the field list for a report hundreds of rows long,
            and scroll it: the rows read ahead come with each answer, and the next Window is asked for before you
            reach the end of the one held.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The shape before the report">
        <p>
            A report source answers the shape of the data it pivots, before any report, with <code>ShapeAsync</code>:
            one over a Snapshot, records' included, answers the Snapshot's own, and a fetching source the one its
            <code>Shape</code> call asks the server for, such as an Arrow schema read by
            <code>SnapshotArrow.ReadShapeAsync</code>. A source that says none answers null. An
            <a href="exboard">ExBoard</a> input is checked against it with <code>BoardInput.CheckAsync</code>, and
            refused by name when a column is missing or of another kind (ADR-0341).
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Caps">
        <p>
            <code>Caps</code> (<code>PivotCaps</code>) refuses, by name, a layout whose report is too large to read:
            more than 200,000 leaves, or more rows or columns than Excel's 1,048,576 and 16,384. The report on screen
            stays, and the Pivot Toolbar says why. ExPivot drawn by the Script Core takes the same caps as
            <code>maxLeaves</code>, <code>maxRows</code> and <code>maxColumns</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Over server data the leaves grow with the product of the placed fields' Items, not with the records, so a
            server's source may say how many Items each field has. Declare it in the features
            (<code>new PivotSourceFeatures(aggregations, canRefresh, countsItems: true)</code>) and pass
            <code>PivotSource.Fetch</code> a fourth delegate, after the other three, that asks for the counts; your server answers it with its own
            source's <code>ItemCountsAsync</code>, a <code>PivotItemCounts</code> under the Source Version it counted,
            written with <code>PivotJson.Write</code>. The bundled sources answer <code>ItemCountsAsync</code> too, so a
            server built on one has nothing to write. <code>PivotLeafBound.Of(layout, counts)</code> is then the most
            leaves a layout can make: the product of its Rows' and Columns' counts, less the Items it hides, or null where
            a field it places was not counted. It is a hint, known before anything is asked, and never a refusal: the
            question is still asked with <code>MaxLeaves</code>, and the server refuses past it as before (ADR-0370).
            In <code>@@ex-grid/pivot-engine</code> they are the third argument of <code>PivotSourceFeatures</code>, an
            <code>itemCounts</code> fetcher beside the other three, each source's <code>itemCounts()</code>, and
            <code>pivotLeafBound</code>.
            The Field List that shows the counts, marks a drop target past the cap and words the refusal by the field
            (<em>Customer (1,020,000 items) in Rows makes more than 200,000 combinations, the most server data allows</em>)
            comes with the TypeScript engine's UI in 0.3.0; ExPivot today does not ask for the counts.
        </p>
        <p id="level-aggregates">
            A collapsed report shows far fewer cells than it has leaves, so a server's source may answer <strong>Level
            Aggregates</strong> in place of a refusal. <code>PivotQuery.ForLevels(layout, maxLeaves)</code> asks the
            question <code>PivotQuery.For</code> asks with the groups the layout shows: <code>Levels</code>, one
            <code>PivotLevelRule</code> a row and a column field, saying whether its Items are collapsed and which are
            toggled. A source that declares it (<code>new PivotSourceFeatures(aggregations, canRefresh, countsItems,
            answersLevels: true)</code>) may answer such a question, when its leaves pass <code>MaxLeaves</code>, with the
            cells of the groups shown instead: each a shown row group crossed with a shown column group, named by its Items
            down to its depth, which a SQL server makes with <code>GROUPING SETS</code>. A cell the levels do not show,
            two cells for one group, and a cell whose parent is missing are each refused by name, as another question's
            answer. A sum that is a double carries the sum of its numbers' magnitudes beside it, so that the report made
            from the cells can check each set of children against its parent within the rounding their order allows,
            and refuse a figure that rounding cannot explain (ADR-0370).
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>// Region collapsed over Product: the grand total and each Region.
// SELECT GROUPING(Region) AS IsTotal, Region, COUNT(*), SUM(Amount) … GROUP BY GROUPING SETS ((), (Region))
var query = PivotQuery.ForLevels(layout, maxLeaves);
var answer = new PivotAnswerBuilder(query, sourceVersion);
foreach (var row in rows)
{
    // A Blank Region is a group of its own, so the total is told by GROUPING(), not by a null.
    var cell = answer.AddCell(row.IsTotal ? [] : [PivotItemKey.For(row.Region)], [], row.Count);
    answer.SetCounts(cell, 0, row.Count, row.Count);
    answer.SetSum(cell, 0, PivotNumber.Exact(row.Amount));   // a double: SetSum(cell, 0, sum, absoluteSum)
}
return answer.Build();</code></pre>
        <p>
            The bundled sources answer Level Aggregates too, without declaring them, and the TypeScript engine's answer
            is theirs cell for cell, so a server built on one has nothing to write. In
            <code>@@ex-grid/pivot-engine</code> they are <code>levelQueryForLayout</code>, the fourth argument of
            <code>PivotSourceFeatures</code>, the builder's <code>addCell</code> and <code>setLevelSum</code>, and an
            answer's <code>isLevels</code>; <code>PivotJson</code> carries a question's <code>levels</code> and a level
            answer's cells, each double sum with its <code>absoluteSum</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The TypeScript engine lays a report out from them. Its cube takes every total as the server answered it and
            checks each set of groups shown below an expanded one against it: the records exactly, and the parts within
            the rounding their order allows. A set that does not add up refuses the whole answer, naming the group
            (<em>The server's answer for [Region=Text:EU] × [] does not add up: its sum of 'Amount' is not what its row
            groups below compose to</em>), and no figure from it is shown. The report is the report the leaves make, to the
            last bit: subtotals, grand totals, Show Values As and sorting by value are computed in the browser from the
            same parts. A <code>LocalPivotReportSource</code> over a <code>FetchingPivotSource</code> that declares
            <code>answersLevels</code> asks every question with the groups its layout shows, and a cube made from Level
            Aggregates (<code>cube.isLevels</code>) holds only those, so an expand asks the server again, one round trip,
            where a cube of leaves already holds it; a collapse of groups it holds is laid out from it. <code>ExPivot.Engine</code>'s cube still refuses an
            answer of cells by name, and ExPivot moves onto the TypeScript engine in 0.3.0, which is why there is no
            Example here.
        </p>
        <p id="level-windows">
            Collapsing does not help where one set of siblings is itself too large: a Customer field of a million Items
            expanded under its Region. Such a set can be answered as a <strong>Sibling Window</strong> when the report sorts it
            by a value. A row field's rule then carries <code>Order</code> (<code>new PivotLevelOrder(value,
            aggregation, descending)</code>, the value in the question's <code>Values</code> and the Aggregation read at
            its grand total), and <code>Levels</code> a <code>PivotLevelWindow(limit, starts)</code>: a set ordered by a
            value with more than <code>limit</code> siblings is answered from the place a <code>PivotWindowStart</code>
            asks under its parent, or the first, with <code>limit</code> siblings and every sibling tied with the first
            and the last, one more each side as a margin, and the siblings beyond each margin merged into one <strong>Rest Cell</strong>
            a column group, which a SQL server makes with <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY … ORDER BY SUM(…))</code>.
            The builder's <code>AddWindow(parentRows, before, after)</code> says how many siblings lie on each side and
            <code>AddRest</code> adds the rest cells; a window not where it was asked, a set past the limit answered
            whole, or a side whose siblings beyond the margin have no rest cell is refused by name, as another question's
            answer. A set sorted by its labels is never windowed and stays refused past the cap, because the labels'
            order (the culture's collation, formatted labels, Order Keys) is not one a server can reproduce, so the
            siblings before a window could not be checked. The bundled sources answer windows too, and
            <code>@@ex-grid/pivot-engine</code>'s answer is theirs to the last bit, as <code>order</code>,
            <code>window</code>, <code>addWindow</code>, <code>addRest</code> and an answer's <code>windows</code> and
            <code>restAt</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The demo API server in this repository, <code>samples/ExGrid.DemoApi</code> over SQLite, declares
            <code>answersLevels</code> and answers both, and its source is a worked example. SQLite has no
            <code>GROUPING SETS</code>, so its aggregate is one <code>GROUP BY</code> a pair of depths joined by
            <code>UNION ALL</code>, each selecting its row and column depths first: a Blank is a <code>NULL</code> too, so
            only the depths say where a group's Items end. A depth's <code>WHERE</code> keeps the records whose Items
            above it are expanded, and a depth that every collapsed Item shuts off is not asked. It answers with the leaves
            whenever they fit, as the bundled sources do. Its windows are cut in .NET rather than with
            <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>: it first reads the row groups at the grand total of the columns, orders each set by its value read from their parts with
            <code>ExGrid.Data</code>'s <code>AggregateArithmetic.Read</code>, the engine's own arithmetic, so an Average
            orders as the report sorts it to the last bit, with ties in its order of Items, and merges the groups of the
            siblings beyond each margin into rest cells. Asked through <code>PivotSource.Fetch</code>, it answers as
            <code>PivotSource.From</code> over the same trades, cell for cell, except for which of two tied siblings beyond
            a window is its margin: the bundled sources take the one their leaves reached first, and the engine holds
            either to the same checks.
        </p>
        <p>
            The TypeScript engine lays a window out with a row for every sibling of its set, so the report's row count and
            the scrollbar drawn from it are the whole set's. Its cube first checks the window: the siblings shown, the
            margins and the rest cells compose to the parent at every column group, and each margin sorts strictly beyond
            its edge, or the answer is refused naming the parent. A sibling outside the window is a
            <strong>Pending Row</strong>: role <code>pending</code>, no label and no value, never painted, and
            <code>valueAt</code>, <code>rowPath</code> and <code>detailsQuery</code> refuse it by name rather than read
            it as empty. Pending Rows are made a few hundred at a time, in the report's slices, so a window over a
            million siblings leaves the page painting while its rows are laid out. A <code>LocalPivotReportSource</code> over a <code>FetchingPivotSource</code> that answers Level
            Aggregates asks a question whole first, and with windows only once the server refuses it past the cap, so a
            report that fits is never windowed. When a report Window reaches Pending Rows it asks again, each set the
            Window reaches started so that the rows asked for lie in the middle of its window, and answers once that
            answer is checked: scrolling down a million customers asks the server for the thousand around the screen, and
            the rows' sequence, and the selection, stay as they were. A Copy or a Selection Summary that reaches a
            Pending Row is refused by name (<code>rowsNotAnswered</code>), never answered with blanks (ADR-0370).
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="From TypeScript">
        <p>
            <code>@@ex-grid/pivot-engine</code>, an npm package that depends only on <code>@@ex-grid/data</code>, is
            ExPivot's engine in TypeScript: the same sources (<code>SnapshotPivotSource</code> over a Snapshot,
            <code>RecordPivotSource</code> over your objects, <code>FetchingPivotSource</code> asking a server), the same
            Leaf Aggregates with decimals summed exactly, and the same report in each form, locale and set of words. It is
            the engine ExPivot's React and Blazor packages move onto (ADR-0370), built in this repository and published
            with 0.3.0; until then ExPivot computes with <code>ExPivot.Engine</code>.
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>import { createPivotLayout, pivotCube, pivotReport, queryForLayout, SnapshotPivotSource } from '@@ex-grid/pivot-engine';

const source = new SnapshotPivotSource(snapshot);   // a Snapshot from @@ex-grid/data
const layout = createPivotLayout({ rows: ['Region'], values: [{ field: 'Amount', aggregation: 'sum' }] });
const query = queryForLayout(layout);
const answer = await source.aggregate(query);        // the Leaf Aggregates, or a refusal (answer.refusal)
const report = pivotReport(pivotCube(query, answer, source.fields), layout, { locale: 'en-US' });</code></pre>
        <p>
            Both engines run the same cases, under <code>tests/cases/pivot/</code>, and a differential run holds whole
            reports over random data to the C# engine, to the last bit of every number. A refusal says the same words,
            except where it names its own platform's call (<code>aggregatePivot</code> for
            <code>PivotEngine.Aggregate</code>). A record behind a cell carries a date as its ticks, as a Snapshot from
            <code>@@ex-grid/data</code> holds one. There is no Example here, because this site is a Blazor app and runs no
            package script of its own.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>PivotJson</code> writes and reads every question and answer a <code>FetchingPivotSource</code> sends its
            server, as the C# engine's <code>PivotJson</code> does, the same document to the byte: the Leaf Aggregates,
            a page of Items or of records, the counts of Items and a notice that the data moved on. A page can so ask a
            server built on <code>ExPivot.Engine</code>, over whatever transport you choose (<code>post</code> here is
            your own, such as a <code>fetch</code> call):
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const source = new FetchingPivotSource(fields, features, {
    aggregate: async (query, signal) => PivotJson.readAnswer(await post('/pivot/aggregate', PivotJson.writeQuery(query), signal)),
    items: async (query, signal) => PivotJson.readItemPage(await post('/pivot/items', PivotJson.writeItemsQuery(query), signal)),
    details: async (query, signal) => PivotJson.readDetailPage(await post('/pivot/details', PivotJson.writeDetailsQuery(query), signal)),
});</code></pre>
        <p>
            An exact number crosses as its digits, without trailing zeros, since a Decimal is a value and not the places
            it was written with; one that no decimal holds exactly, past 28 places or past 2^96, is refused by name
            rather than rounded, by either engine. A document of a version the reader does not read is refused with a
            <code>PivotVersionError</code>, and one that cannot be with a <code>PivotFormatError</code> naming what is
            wrong.
        </p>
        <p>
            A <code>SnapshotPivotSource</code> is live as the C# one is (<a href="expivot/live">Live data</a>):
            <code>apply(batch)</code> takes a Change Batch, folds it into the answer held for the last question, and tells
            each listener given to <code>subscribe</code> the new Source Version. Its work runs in slices of 30 ms by
            default, yielding to the browser between them, and a question whose <code>AbortSignal</code> is cancelled
            rejects with the signal's reason at the next slice:
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const source = new SnapshotPivotSource(snapshot, undefined, { sliceBudgetMs: 30 });
const stop = source.subscribe(({ sourceVersion }) => refresh(sourceVersion));
source.apply(createChangeBatch({ changed, removedKeys: [42n] }));    // from @@ex-grid/data
const answer = await source.aggregate(query, controller.signal);</code></pre>
        <p>
            After a batch it reads the records in the Snapshot's order, so its answer is the one a new source over the
            next Snapshot gives, leaves and Items in the same order. The C# source reads them in the order its storage
            keeps, which a changed record moves until the Snapshot is compacted: the two hold the same leaves, Items and
            exact numbers, and a sum of doubles may differ in its last bit where the order changed it. The shared cases
            and a differential run over random batches hold both.
        </p>
        <p>
            A <code>FetchingPivotSource</code> is live too, over a transport you own: a WebSocket, Server-Sent Events,
            SignalR or a poll, whatever your server already speaks. Give its fetchers a <code>subscribe</code> that starts
            your transport, hands each message on to <code>notify</code>, and returns the function that stops it; the source
            starts it when its first listener subscribes and stops it when its last one goes. A message is one of two
            notices. <code>changed</code> says the data moved on and asks the question again, as AG Grid's
            <code>refreshServerSide</code> does. <code>changeBatch</code> names the records the data lost and gained between
            two Source Versions, each as its values in the order of the fields, as AG Grid's
            <code>applyServerSideTransaction</code> does; the engine finds the leaves they fall in and folds them into the
            answer it holds, so a total moves without a round trip:
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const source = new FetchingPivotSource(fields, features, {
    aggregate, items, details,
    subscribe: notify => {
        const socket = new WebSocket('/pivot/live');                  // your transport
        socket.onmessage = event => notify(readNotice(event.data));  // your message to a notice
        return () => socket.close();
    },
});
// { kind: 'changed', sourceVersion: 'v8' }
// { kind: 'changeBatch', from: 'v8', to: 'v9',
//   removed: [['East', 'FX', { mantissa: 1000n, scale: 2 }]], added: [['East', 'FX', { mantissa: 1250n, scale: 2 }]] }</code></pre>
        <p>
            A count is folded, and so is an exact sum and an extreme numbers only came to. Anything the records alone
            cannot carry is asked of the server again rather than guessed: a sum of doubles, a product, a variance, an
            extreme a number left, a number that is not finite, a batch that does not start where the last one ended, a
            record whose leaf the answer does not hold, and an answer of Level Aggregates. A malformed notice throws to your
            transport and changes nothing. A batch is folded when the question is next asked, so a burst of them costs one
            fold. An answer is held only while your transport runs, and Refresh asks the server (a source that wraps
            another passes its <code>refresh</code> on). A changed record is the old one removed and the new one added. A record
            carries its values as a record behind a cell does: an exact number as a <code>SnapshotDecimal</code>, a date as
            its ticks, and a date part as its number; a JavaScript number is a double, whose sum is asked again. A <code>LocalPivotReportSource</code> over the source tells
            its own listeners each notice, as it does over a Snapshot.
        </p>
        <p>
            ExPivot drawn by the Script Core (<code>createPivot</code>, or <code>ExPivot</code> from
            <code>@@ex-grid/pivot-react</code>) hears those notices itself and asks the report on screen again, under its
            layout, as the Blazor component does: a notice after a quiet spell at once, and the notices that come within
            <code>redrawInterval</code> of the last change shown (250 ms unless given) gathered into one question, asked
            when the interval ends. A notice of the version already on screen asks nothing (unless the report is stale,
            below), and a question out is never
            cancelled by one: its answer is shown, and the changes that came meanwhile are asked for after it. Such a
            question is asked quietly: the pivot is not marked busy for it, so a ticking report does not flicker.
        </p>
        <p>
            Newer data the report cannot show leaves a Stale Report, as on the
            <a href="expivot/live">Live data</a> page: an answer past <code>maxLeaves</code>, a refusal or a failure of
            the source keeps the report on the last data it could show, with its numbers muted, and a notice under the
            Pivot Toolbar says as of when and why, with Retry, which asks the source again. While it is stale, Copy is
            refused, saying the time the data is as of. The next answer for newer data, or Retry's, ends it. A failure of the
            source is raised to <code>onFailed</code>; one the notice says is thrown no further.
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const pivot = createPivot(element, {
    source,                       // a SnapshotPivotSource, or a FetchingPivotSource with a transport
    layout,
    redrawInterval: 1000,         // a ticking source redraws the report at most once a second
});</code></pre>
        <p>
            The cube and the report run in slices too. <code>pivotCubeAsync</code> and <code>pivotReportAsync</code> take
            the same slicing and an <code>AbortSignal</code>, and give what <code>pivotCube</code> and
            <code>pivotReport</code> give, so an answer of a hundred thousand leaves, or a field with thousands of Items
            to order, leaves the page painting while its report is made. A report's <code>hasSameRowsAsAsync</code>
            compares its rows with another's in slices the same way. Each call begins a slice of its own and ends
            without yielding, so yield between them where the page must paint:
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const cube = await pivotCubeAsync(query, answer, source.fields, { sliceBudgetMs: 30 }, controller.signal);
const report = await pivotReportAsync(cube, layout, { locale: 'en-US' }, { sliceBudgetMs: 30 }, controller.signal);
const keepState = await report.hasSameRowsAsAsync(shown, { sliceBudgetMs: 30 }, controller.signal);</code></pre>
        <p>
            <code>LocalPivotReportSource</code> is the report source over any of them, as <code>PivotReportSource.From</code>
            is in C#: asked for a Window of the report a layout makes, it answers that Window's rows with the whole report's
            metadata, under a Report Version that Copy, the Selection Summary, a field's Items and Show Details name back,
            and it marks each cell a Change Batch moved with the version that moved it. Everything it answers is plain data,
            so a Window crosses to a Web Worker as it is. Unlike the C# source, it always answers a Window whole: Window
            Changes and their digest were the server's answer to a round trip, which the browser does not make. A layout
            gesture (<code>markChanges: false</code>) lays out the data of the Window named as its <code>baseline</code>,
            so a change you have not shown yet is marked by the next request that marks changes. The settings name their
            <code>locale</code>; with none, the browser's language is taken. When a highlight starts is yours, on your clock:
            <code>PivotChangeTimes</code> stamps each change the first time you adopt a Window that lists it.
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>const reports = new LocalPivotReportSource(source, { orderKeys: { tenor } });
const shown = await reports.window({
    requestId: crypto.randomUUID(), layout, window: { start: 0, count: 60 },
    settings: { locale: 'en-US', orderKeyPolicies: { Tenor: 'tenor' } },
    baseline: held?.metadata.version,
});
if (shown.refusal === undefined) {
    times.adopt(shown, Date.now(), 1000);            // times = new PivotChangeTimes()
    const copy = await reports.copy({ version: shown.metadata.version, columns, ranges });
}</code></pre>
        <p>
            Over a <code>SnapshotPivotSource</code>, a Window after a Change Batch is computed from what the batch touched,
            as the C# source computes it (ADR-0153): the leaves it moved, the totals above them, and the rows that show
            them, so one record changed among a hundred thousand costs a few tens of milliseconds, not a whole report.
            Every other row is the very object the Window before held. The result is the report a fresh source over the
            same Snapshot makes, to the last bit of every number. Once a batch has begun to be folded in, the work is
            carried through to its end, and a cancellation is heard only before that, or while a new layout is laid out.
        </p>
